# Line endings in the files that the tests read.
#
# The sim hashes a state and compares the string against a recorded one. A
# Windows checkout converts a text file to CRLF by default. That conversion
# changes the bytes, so a recorded hash stops matching, and the failure looks
# like a defect in the hash rather than a setting in the checkout.
#
# Pin the text files to LF on every platform. This is not a preference about
# editors. It is the condition under which the tests mean anything on Windows.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonc text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Binary, and never converted.
#
# A line ending conversion corrupts each of these in silence.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
